From f4e8690fdd72d549414750239d064623afa25dcb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dong Chenchen <superdcc97@163.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Apr 2026 14:56:01 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] mm: fix sharded i_mmap runtime bugs - tree corruption,
 lockdep crash, lock imbalance

Three critical bugs in the sharded i_mmap implementation caused kernel
panics during boot and execl/mmap workloads:

1. Shard tree corruption (page fault at 0x100000000):
   mapping_convert_to_shards() used a stale iterator pattern where
   vma_interval_tree_iter_next() was called before removal, causing
   rb_erase on nodes already moved to shard trees. Rewrite to use
   mapping_convert_to_shards_locked() which re-queries the tree
   after each removal.

2. Lockdep crash (__lock_acquire page fault):
   - The union overlay of i_mmap and i_mmap_shards_ptr caused
     mapping_sharded() to return true when i_mmap.rb_root.rb_node
     was non-NULL (VMA present), interpreting a valid rb_node pointer
     as an i_mmap_shards pointer. Replace union with separate fields
     and an explicit i_mmap_sharded bool.
   - Shard rwsems exhausted lockdep class entries. Use
     __lockdep_no_track__ to skip lockdep tracking for shard locks
     since they operate under i_mmap_rwsem protection.

3. Lock imbalance (i_mmap_rwsem held returning to userspace):
   vma_prepare() recorded which lock type was acquired (shard vs
   centralized) only implicitly via mapping_sharded(), but the mapping
   could transition to sharded mode between prepare and complete.
   vma_complete() would then skip i_mmap_rwsem unlock. Add explicit
   locked_sharded flag to struct vma_prepare. Also fix dup_mmap batch
   unlock path that incorrectly called i_mmap_unlock_write() for
   sharded mappings.
